.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]event\f[R] struct and its \f[CR]command\f[R] string are owned
by the library and freed after the callback returns.
Copy with \f[CR]strdup()\f[R] if needed beyond the callback scope.
.IP \(bu 2
Break\-hit events are generated for breakpoint matches \f[B]and\f[R]
after step/next/finish operations.
The callback is the unified entry point for all debugger stops.
.IP \(bu 2
It is safe to call debug control functions
(\f[CR]bc_debug_continue\f[R], \f[CR]bc_debug_step\f[R], etc.)
from within the callback.
The server queues the command and resumes after \f[CR]bc_poll()\f[R]
returns.
.IP \(bu 2
If no resumption command is issued, the debugger remains paused.
Subsequent \f[CR]bc_poll()\f[R] calls will block (up to timeout) waiting
for events that will not arrive until execution resumes.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]depth\f[R] field helps distinguish between top\-level commands
and commands inside functions, subshells, or loops.
.IP \(bu 2
Registering a callback does not enable the debugger.
Call \f[CR]bc_debug_enable()\f[R] and add breakpoints separately.
.IP \(bu 2
The callback persists across \f[CR]bc_debug_disable()\f[R] /
\f[CR]bc_debug_enable()\f[R] cycles.
.IP \(bu 2
Thread safety: callbacks are always invoked from the thread that calls
\f[CR]bc_poll()\f[R].
The library is not thread\-safe.
